nd it's  something we seriously considered and we  actually had all levels of options as to  having some fans present having no  general fans and spectators present but  allowing necessary personnel maybe even  guests of the players and coaches and  even if you take out the guests of the  players and coaches and you have  necessary personnel and teams and all  that gets involved when you're bringing  8 teams to a campus we quickly exceeded  the limits that our institutions have  put in place for other events and the  way we approached athletics in the Ivy  League is that student-athletes are  treated like other students so it seemed  inconsistent to the presidents to have a  policy that applied to the campus at  large and then not apply it to athletics  so what about the concept of sending  Yale and Princeton on to the tournament  writ large where it appears they're  excellent question made that for all of  the winter sports where the seasons had  concluded the regular season that  concluded and to the extent teams and  individual to qualified for NCAA or  other postseason events each institution  could decide how to approach the whether  or not to approve that competition and  so those events are different than  bringing in a team to an Ivy League  campus and particularly to basketball  the NCAA provides charter airplanes  which is a different mode of  transportation than having to fly  commercially and so each institution  will have to make the decision whether  or not to send their teams or  student-athletes to the NCAA but the  presidents were comfortable because that  wasn't violating any campus policy  regarding large gathering interesting or  do you have any information as to how  Yale and Princeton is handling because  right now the NCAA is planning on having  the NCAA tournament in front of fans as  it's currently you and I sitting right  now about an hour after the World Health  Organization officially labeled kovat 19  pandemic right now as we are talking